WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.108
Picture moving house.

2
00:00:04.108 --> 00:00:09.540
You pack your books into twenty boxes, and label each one: books, authors A to C.

3
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.896
To find a cookbook by Moreno, you open one box.

4
00:00:12.896 --> 00:00:17.316
A friend packed hers in any order, and labelled every box: books.

5
00:00:17.316 --> 00:00:18.744
She opens all twenty.

6
00:00:18.933 --> 00:00:21.796
Data files have the same problem.

7
00:00:21.796 --> 00:00:25.353
Two files hold the same 400,000 payments.

8
00:00:25.353 --> 00:00:29.257
Ask both for every payment above 2,400 euros.

9
00:00:29.467 --> 00:00:32.899
From one, the reader fetches 383 kilobytes.

10
00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:35.294
From the other, 2.3 megabytes.

11
00:00:35.294 --> 00:00:37.768
The same 16,200 rows come back.

12
00:00:37.768 --> 00:00:40.562
Only the order of the rows differs.

13
00:00:40.562 --> 00:00:43.196
So what is the reader looking at?

14
00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:44.998
These are Parquet files.

15
00:00:44.998 --> 00:00:49.299
Parquet is a file format that stores a table column by column.

16
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:55.054
The rows are cut into blocks called row groups, here four of about 100,000 rows.

17
00:00:55.054 --> 00:00:59.220
Inside a row group, each column sits together, as one chunk.

18
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:08.047
A fraud analyst, someone who hunts for stolen cards, asks for three columns of every payment above 1,500 euros.

19
00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:11.163
The reader skips the two columns she didn't name.

20
00:01:11.163 --> 00:01:12.479
That's column pruning.

21
00:01:12.479 --> 00:01:15.229
But can it skip rows too, before reading them?

22
00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:16.949
It can, with the footer.

23
00:01:16.949 --> 00:01:25.665
At the end of the file, the footer lists every chunk: where it starts, how big it is, and its smallest and largest value, its min and max.

24
00:01:25.833 --> 00:01:27.590
Remember the moving boxes?

25
00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:31.777
The footer is the label from every box, copied onto one sheet.

26
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.146
So the reader reads the footer first.

27
00:01:36.146 --> 00:01:45.784
In this file, sorted by amount, row group zero holds amounts from 100 to 63,031 cents.

28
00:01:45.784 --> 00:01:49.033
Row group one, up to 125,677.

29
00:01:49.033 --> 00:01:51.162
Two, up to 188,240.

30
00:01:51.162 --> 00:01:53.964
And three, up to 250,099.

31
00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:57.253
The analyst wants amounts above 150,000 cents.

32
00:01:57.253 --> 00:02:01.983
Which row groups can the reader skip, without reading a byte of them?

33
00:02:01.983 --> 00:02:03.080
Pause and guess.

34
00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:07.245
Zero and one.

35
00:02:07.245 --> 00:02:12.260
Their largest amounts are below 150,000, so no row in them can match.

36
00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:15.749
Two and three might, so the reader fetches them.

37
00:02:15.933 --> 00:02:22.600
Six chunks, 788,725 bytes of the file's 3.6 million.

38
00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:24.395
About a fifth.

39
00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:29.226
Min and max work on whole row groups, though.

40
00:02:29.226 --> 00:02:34.160
Row group two starts at 125,677, below the line.

41
00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:45.057
So the reader unpacks all 199,296 rows of groups two and three, and a filter keeps the 160,308 that match.

42
00:02:45.233 --> 00:02:51.688
Checking a filter against these numbers, inside the file reader, is called predicate pushdown.

43
00:02:51.688 --> 00:02:55.396
A predicate is a condition, like amount above 150,000.

44
00:02:55.396 --> 00:02:59.653
Pushing it down means handing it from the query to the reader.

45
00:02:59.833 --> 00:03:01.263
Now the other file.

46
00:03:01.263 --> 00:03:05.026
Same payments, written in the order they happened.

47
00:03:05.026 --> 00:03:09.240
Every row group holds amounts from 100 to about 250,000.

48
00:03:09.433 --> 00:03:11.799
Ask for amounts above 240,000.

49
00:03:11.799 --> 00:03:14.954
How many row groups can the reader skip?

50
00:03:14.954 --> 00:03:16.216
Pause and guess.

51
00:03:19.433 --> 00:03:19.824
None.

52
00:03:19.824 --> 00:03:21.701
Every label says: maybe.

53
00:03:21.701 --> 00:03:25.768
That's the friend's boxes, every one labelled books.

54
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:32.641
The reader unpacks all 400,000 rows, and the filter throws away 96% of them.

55
00:03:32.867 --> 00:03:39.996
On the sorted file, it reads one row group of four, 98,944 rows.

56
00:03:39.996 --> 00:03:42.224
Same 16,200 answers.

57
00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.161
Pushdown was on both times.

58
00:03:44.161 --> 00:03:46.509
It just had nothing to push against.

59
00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:49.972
So, min and max skip only what sorting made tidy.

60
00:03:49.972 --> 00:03:53.177
Sort each file by the column you filter on most.

61
00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.865
But one sort tidies one column.

62
00:03:55.865 --> 00:04:02.544
In the sorted file, the merchant column still runs from 0 to 499 in every row group.

63
00:04:02.733 --> 00:04:04.761
And here the moving boxes break.

64
00:04:04.761 --> 00:04:07.677
You can repack a box when you notice the mess.

65
00:04:07.677 --> 00:04:10.845
A Parquet file is never changed once it's written.

66
00:04:10.845 --> 00:04:13.000
To tidy one, you write a new file.

67
00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:19.031
The moral: a label only helps if it's narrow, and the order of the rows decides how narrow.

68
00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:22.004
One quick check, on the sorted file.

69
00:04:22.004 --> 00:04:23.882
Amounts below 50,000 cents.

70
00:04:23.882 --> 00:04:26.734
How many row groups does the reader open?

71
00:04:26.734 --> 00:04:27.847
Pause and think.

72
00:04:31.067 --> 00:04:31.536
One.

73
00:04:31.536 --> 00:04:33.295
Row group zero.

74
00:04:33.295 --> 00:04:36.932
Row group one starts at 63,033.
